Binary Search is a search algorithm that finds a specific element in a sorted array. It works by progressively halving the search range to quickly locate the target value. Binary search has a time complexity of O(log n), which makes it very efficient when dealing with large-scale data.

Below is a sample code showing how to implement the binary search algorithm in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int binarySearch(const std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {
int left = 0; // Left boundary
int right = arr.size() - 1; // Right boundary

while (left <= right) { // Continue searching while left boundary is less than or equal to right boundary
int mid = left + (right - left) / 2; // Calculate middle position, avoid overflow

if (arr[mid] == target) { // If middle value equals target value
return mid; // Return index of middle value
} else if (arr[mid] < target) { // If middle value is less than target value
left = mid + 1; // Continue searching in right half
} else { // If middle value is greater than target value
right = mid - 1; // Continue searching in left half
}
}

return -1; // If target value is not found, return -1
}

int main() {
std::vector<int> arr = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15};
int target = 7;

int result = binarySearch(arr, target);

if (result != -1) {
std::cout << "Target value " << target << " index in array is: " << result << std::endl;
} else {
std::cout << "Target value " << target << " is not in array" << std::endl;
}

return 0;
}
```

### Code explanation:
1. **`int left = 0;`**: Initialize the left boundary as the starting position of the array.
2. **`int right = arr.size() - 1;`**: Initialize the right boundary as the end position of the array.
3. **`while (left <= right)`**: Continue searching while the left boundary is less than or equal to the right boundary.
4. **`int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;`**: Calculate the middle position, avoid overflow.
5. **`if (arr[mid] == target)`**: If the middle value equals the target value, return the index of the middle value.
6. **`else if (arr[mid] < target)`**: If the middle value is less than the target value, continue searching in the right half.
7. **`else`**: If the middle value is greater than the target value, continue searching in the left half.
8. **`return -1;`**: If the target value is not found, return -1.

